<li><a href="#concepts"><span>01</span><strong>Core concepts</strong><small>What makes this Prismatic?</small></a></li>
<li><a href="#model"><span>02</span><strong>Objects &amp; mathematics</strong><small>Define the material and its behavior.</small></a></li>
<li><a href="#style-guide"><span>03</span><strong>Style guide</strong><small>Color, type, geometry, restraint.</small></a></li>
<li><a href="#components"><span>04</span><strong>Components</strong><small>Derive the family from the rules.</small></a></li>
<li><a href="#information"><span>05</span><strong>Information patterns</strong><small>Give the light a reading order.</small></a></li>
<li><a href="#use"><span>06</span><strong>Build with it</strong><small>Recipes, limits, and source.</small></a></li>
</ol></nav><section id="concepts" class="guide-chapter" aria-labelledby="concepts-title"><header><div><p class="prism-eyebrow">01 / CORE CONCEPTS</p><h2 id="concepts-title">One light. A family of objects.</h2></div><p>Prismatic makes relationships visible: colors belong to an anchor, objects have a degree of permanence, and their light comes from one place.</p></header><div class="guide-concepts">
<article class="prism-card"><p class="prism-eyebrow">I1 / DERIVE THE SPECTRUM</p><div class="guide-bands" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><h3>One hue, related bands.</h3><p>The anchor is a parameter, not a bag of swatches. Offsets derive a family of colors. Semantic success, warning, and danger keep their own fixed hue angles.</p></article>
<article class="prism-card" style="box-shadow:var(--elev-dense)"><p class="prism-eyebrow">I2 / GIVE PERMANENCE WEIGHT</p><h3>Light becomes substance.</h3><p>Transient objects float. Standing objects settle. Structural and destructive actions become dense. Material treatment tells you what kind of object you are dealing with.</p><p class="guide-contract">transient → standing → immovable</p></article>
<article class="prism-card"><p class="prism-eyebrow">I3 / SHARE THE LIGHT</p><h3>A common reference.</h3><div class="prism-holo" aria-hidden="true"></div><p>Edges, casts, and foil read the same light variables. Moving the pointer changes their relationship together; each object interprets the reference through its own material rules.</p></article>
</div><div class="guide-invariant"><strong>THE IDENTITY TEST</strong><p>Rotate the hue and the system should remain recognizable: chromatic edges, density that signals permanence, and objects that share a light source. The optical language is a design metaphor, not a physical optics simulation.</p></div></section><section id="model" class="guide-chapter" aria-labelledby="model-title"><header><div><p class="prism-eyebrow">02 / OBJECTS &amp; MATHEMATICS</p><h2 id="model-title">Define the object. Derive the behavior.</h2></div><p>The vocabulary is finite. Hue, light, and timing provide measured variation inside it. Each formula below corresponds to the implementation on this page.</p></header><div class="guide-table-wrap"><table class="guide-table"><caption>Object contract / discrete choices before continuous parameters</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Axis</th><th scope="col">Allowed vocabulary</th><th scope="col">What it controls</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Mode</th><td>day | night</td><td>Figure and ground, cast versus halo, filled versus outlined display type.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Density</th><td>transient | standing | immovable</td><td>The relative weight of edge, wash, fill, and shadow.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Role</th><td>brand | info | success | warning | danger</td><td>Brand and info follow the anchor. Other semantic hues remain fixed.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Geometry</th><td>button: xs–xl; badge/chip: sm–lg</td><td>A finite size family; the dichroic edge stays 1.5px.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Reference</th><td>angle, vector, intensity, position</td><td>Six shared numbers drive the light. Consumers read CSS custom properties.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p class="guide-contract">Object = (mode, density, role, geometry) + shared light reference</p><p>This is the design contract. CSS classes, semantic tokens, and the root mode implement it; it is not a separate widget configuration API.</p>
<div class="guide-equations">
<article class="prism-card"><h3>Color is a derived coordinate.</h3><code>Hᵢ = wrap(H + Δᵢ, 360°)<br>colorᵢ = oklch(Lᵢ Cᵢ Hᵢ)</code><p>Day offsets: 0°, −35°, +75°, −140°. Night offsets: 0°, −25°, +55°, −95°. Each band has its own lightness and chroma. Hue rotation alone is not a contrast guarantee.</p><p id="guide-hue-equation" class="guide-contract">Day: H = 185° → bands 185°, 150°, 260°, 45°</p></article>
<article class="prism-card"><h3>Updates have a defined resolution.</h3><code>Q(x, δ) = δ · round(x / δ)<br>δangle = 3° · δvector = 0.02<br>δintensity = 0.05 · δposition = 2%</code><p>Write a light variable when it crosses a step, at most once per animation frame. CSS interpolates between steps. Quantization limits updates; it does not eliminate the cost of rendering more objects.</p></article>
<article class="prism-card"><h3>Night uses an art-directed hue map.</h3><code>h ∈ [0°, 360°]<br>R(h) = h, if h ≤ 45<br>R(h) = round(h − 0.4(h − 45)), if 45 &lt; h ≤ 300<br>R(h) = round((h + 0.4(385 − h)) mod 360), otherwise</code><p>At the default h = 185°, R(h) = 129°. This piecewise mapping intentionally changes branches above 300°; it is not a smooth wavelength conversion. Night also changes lightness, chroma, and band offsets.</p></article>
<article class="prism-card"><h3>Timing comes from a small scale.</h3><code>q = 40ms · duration = n · q<br>flash 3q · arrive 6q · settle 10q<br>shadow lag 2q · mode change 25q</code><p>Press-in is the explicit 1.5q exception: 60ms. Three easing curves separate light, mass, and refraction. Reduced motion removes animation and transition time.</p></article>
</div><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-holography">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">LAW 21 · HOLOGRAPHY</span>
          <h3 id="ds-holography">Holography — one light, cascaded by reference</h3>
          <p>
            The pointer is the light source, and the whole page is the hologram. Six
            registered custom properties on the root — cast angle, cast vector, intensity,
            position — form the <em>reference beam</em>; every dichroic edge, chromatic
            cast, glint and tilt derives from them in pure CSS <code class="prism-data">calc()</code>.
            The driver writes the root at most once per frame, and only when a value
            crosses its quantum (Δ3° angle, Δ0.02 vector) — most frames write nothing,
            and the typed properties refract between quanta on their own. The light driver updates shared references; each material consumer reads them through CSS.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--2" style="align-items: start;">
          <div class="ds-stack">
            <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--2">
              <div class="prism-holo">
                <p class="prism-eyebrow">Foil</p>
                <p class="prism-ui" style="margin-top: 0.4rem;">Spectrum = <span class="prism-data">--la</span></p>
              </div>
              <div class="prism-holo">
                <p class="prism-eyebrow">Glint</p>
                <p class="prism-ui" style="margin-top: 0.4rem;">Highlight = <span class="prism-data">--lxp/--lyp</span></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="ds-caption">
              Foil chips: gradient angle, glint position and ±3° tilt are all var() reads.
              No per-card light listener. More objects still add style, paint, and compositing work.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="prism-card" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Reference monitor · live</p>
            <p class="prism-data ds-holo-mono" aria-live="off">
              --la <b id="ds-ref-la">135</b>° · --lv <b id="ds-ref-v">0.71, 0.71</b> · --li <b id="ds-ref-li">0.35</b>
            </p>
            <p class="prism-data ds-holo-mono">
              frames <b id="ds-ref-frames">0</b> · write batches <b id="ds-ref-writes">0</b> · quanta Δ3° / Δ0.02 / Δ0.05 / Δ2%
            </p>
            <p class="prism-body prism-muted" style="margin-top: 0.9rem;">
              This readout is the entire runtime contract: the beam is written, the cascade
              does the rest. Write batches never exceed frames, and once the pointer settles
              they stop — quantization avoids repeated light-variable writes at rest. At night the casts are
              already halos, so only the glint and the foil answer the light: the material rules stay consistent.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-redshift">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">LAW 16–19 · REDSHIFT</span>
          <h3 id="ds-redshift">Redshift — reinterpret the same roles at night</h3>
          <p>
            Toggle the mode (top right). Night recomputes the same roles: a dark field, a light surface,
            edge↔fill, cast→halo, filled display→outline. An art-directed hue map drives <code class="prism-data">redshift(h)</code>. Mode changes take 25q; reduced motion removes the transition.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--2" style="align-items: start;">
          <div class="prism-card prism-edge">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Figure / ground swap</p>
            <p class="prism-body" style="color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0.5rem 0 0;">
              In day this card is paper on a near-white field with a dichroic edge. In night
              the field goes dark, this surface becomes the paper figure, and the edge inverts
              to quiet while the body carries the spectrum.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="prism-card ds-stack" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Conserved palette</p>
            <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--4">
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--anchor);"></span>
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--near);"></span>
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--far);"></span>
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--counter);"></span>
            </div>
            <p class="ds-caption">Same bands, recomputed from <code class="prism-data">--hr</code> at night.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-motion">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">LAW 8–15 · MOTION + QUANTUM</span>
          <h3 id="ds-motion">Motion — quantized, massless, causal</h3>
          <p>
            q&nbsp;=&nbsp;40ms; durations use integer multiples except the 1.5q press. Three easing curves, no springs.
            Properties animate brightness → form → shadow, and the shadow trails its object by
            2q. Unknown state is superposed; entangled elements break the lag.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--2">
          <!-- Stagger -->
          <div class="prism-card" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <div class="ds-row-actions" style="justify-content: space-between;">
              <p class="prism-eyebrow">Group entrance · 1q/child, cap 6</p>
              <button class="ds-replay" data-replay="stagger" type="button">Replay</button>
            </div>
            <div id="ds-stagger" class="prism-stagger ds-grid ds-grid--3" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--anchor);"></span>
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--near);"></span>
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--far);"></span>
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--counter);"></span>
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--info-dense);"></span>
              <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--dense);"></span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Superposition -->
          <div class="prism-card" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <div class="ds-row-actions" style="justify-content: space-between;">
              <p class="prism-eyebrow">Superposition · collapse 5q</p>
              <button class="ds-replay" data-replay="collapse" type="button">Observe</button>
            </div>
            <div class="ds-stack" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
              <div class="prism-ghost" data-ghost style="height: 1.4rem;"></div>
              <div class="prism-ghost" data-ghost style="height: 1.4rem; width: 80%;"></div>
              <div class="prism-ghost" data-ghost style="height: 1.4rem; width: 60%;"></div>
            </div>
            <div id="ds-collapsed" hidden style="margin-top: 1rem;">
              <p class="prism-data">↳ run 0xA2 · hue 150° · status ok</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Entanglement -->
          <div class="prism-card" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Entanglement · Δt = 0 on shared datum</p>
            <p class="prism-body" style="color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0.4rem 0 1rem;">
              Hover a dot or its row — both light instantly. Unrelated neighbors still lag 2q.
            </p>
            <div class="ds-row-actions" style="gap: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
              <span class="prism-dot" data-pair="r1" tabindex="0" aria-label="datum r1"></span>
              <span class="prism-dot" data-pair="r2" tabindex="0" aria-label="datum r2"></span>
              <span class="prism-dot" data-pair="r3" tabindex="0" aria-label="datum r3"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="prism-table" role="table" style="--cols: 1fr 1fr;">
              <div class="prism-row" role="row" data-pair="r1"><span>r1</span><span>185°</span></div>
              <div class="prism-row" role="row" data-pair="r2"><span>r2</span><span>150°</span></div>
              <div class="prism-row" role="row" data-pair="r3"><span>r3</span><span>027°</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Light order -->
          <div class="prism-card" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <div class="ds-row-actions" style="justify-content: space-between;">
              <p class="prism-eyebrow">Light order · enter from −10,−12</p>
              <button class="ds-replay" data-replay="enter" type="button">Replay</button>
            </div>
            <div id="ds-enter" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
              <div class="prism-card prism-edge" style="margin: 0;">
                <p class="prism-body" style="margin: 0;">Arrives from the light source, decelerates, then its shadow settles 2q behind.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section></section><section id="style-guide" class="guide-chapter" aria-labelledby="style-guide-title"><header><div><p class="prism-eyebrow">03 / STYLE GUIDE</p><h2 id="style-guide-title">Make the vocabulary consistent.</h2></div><p>Color roles, type roles, and geometry turn the optical idea into repeatable choices. Use the hue and mode controls to inspect those choices together.</p></header><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-dispersion">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">LAW 1–2 · COLOR</span>
          <h3 id="ds-dispersion">Dispersion</h3>
          <p>
            Brand bands sit at fixed angular offsets from the anchor (+0, &minus;35, +75,
            &minus;140), all held in L&nbsp;0.70&ndash;0.78 / C&nbsp;0.11&ndash;0.14 for the day palette. Night uses its own offsets and L/C values. Verify foreground and background pairs together; the palette alone does not guarantee contrast.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--4" aria-label="Brand bands">
          <div class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--anchor);"><span class="ds-swatch-label">anchor<small data-band-offset="0">+0°</small></span></div>
          <div class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--near);"><span class="ds-swatch-label">near<small data-band-offset="1">&minus;35°</small></span></div>
          <div class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--far);"><span class="ds-swatch-label">far<small data-band-offset="2">+75°</small></span></div>
          <div class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--counter);"><span class="ds-swatch-label">counter<small data-band-offset="3">&minus;140°</small></span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--4" style="margin-top: 1rem;" aria-label="Role tokens">
          <div class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--field); color:var(--on-ground)"><span class="ds-swatch-label">field<small>ground</small></span></div>
          <div class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--surface);"><span class="ds-swatch-label">surface<small>raised</small></span></div>
          <div class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--ink); color: var(--surface);"><span class="ds-swatch-label">ink<small>text</small></span></div>
          <div class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--dense); color: var(--on-dense);"><span class="ds-swatch-label">dense<small>immovable</small></span></div>
        </div>
      </section><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-semantics">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">STYLE / SEMANTIC HUES</span>
          <h3 id="ds-semantics">Semantics — fixed hue angles</h3>
          <p>
            success&nbsp;152°, danger&nbsp;27°, warning&nbsp;75° are pinned in both modes —
            they never rotate with the brand. Info alone inherits the anchor. Only the shade
            adapts at night. State is never hue-only: badges carry words.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--4">
          <div class="prism-card ds-stack" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--success);">success<small> 152°</small></span>
            <span class="prism-badge prism-badge--ok">✓ Approved</span>
          </div>
          <div class="prism-card ds-stack" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--danger);">danger<small> 27°</small></span>
            <span class="prism-badge prism-badge--no">✕ Rejected</span>
          </div>
          <div class="prism-card ds-stack" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--warning);">warning<small> 75°</small></span>
            <span class="prism-badge prism-badge--warn">! Pending</span>
          </div>
          <div class="prism-card ds-stack" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <span class="prism-swatch" style="background: var(--info);">info<small> = anchor</small></span>
            <span class="prism-badge" style="background: var(--info-dense); color: var(--on-info);">i Info</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-type">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">LAW 20 · TYPE</span>
          <h3 id="ds-type">Type — seven roles, line work on top</h3>
          <p>
            Display is constructed line work (Tilt&nbsp;Prism), allowed only ≥40px — below
            that, use Instrument&nbsp;Sans 600. Day fills it; night outlines it.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="prism-card ds-type-stack" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
          <div>
            <span class="ds-type-meta">display · line work · ≥40px</span>
            <p class="prism-display">Refraction</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="ds-type-meta">headline · Instrument Sans 600</span>
            <p class="prism-headline">Light splits into spectra at the boundary.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="ds-type-meta">body · Sans 400</span>
            <p class="prism-body" style="margin: 0;">The reading ground stays quiet. Color concentrates at edges, status, and dense actions.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="ds-type-meta">data · Spline Sans Mono</span>
            <p class="prism-data" style="margin: 0;">--h: 185 → --hr: 129 · q = 40ms · AA ≥ 4.5:1</p>
          </div>
          <div><span class="ds-type-meta">UI · Sans 500 · 13.5px</span><p class="prism-ui">Inspect release · Save changes</p></div>
          <div><span class="ds-type-meta">eyebrow · Mono · 12px</span><p class="prism-eyebrow">04 / COMPONENT FAMILY</p></div>
          <div>
            <span class="ds-type-meta">aside · Instrument Serif italic · one phrase</span>
            <p class="prism-aside" style="margin: 0;">the human voice, used sparingly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section><div class="guide-table-wrap"><table class="guide-table"><caption>Geometry / choose the family before the size</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Family</th><th scope="col">Defined values</th><th scope="col">Usage rule</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Radius</th><td>10px / 16px / 24px</td><td>Default control / foil / surface. Button size variants use 7, 8, 10, 12, and 14px.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Boundary</th><td>1.5px dichroic edge / 1px data hairline / 2px row seam</td><td>Keep these roles distinct; increasing every edge weakens hierarchy.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Spacing</th><td>1rem component gap / 1.5rem group gap / 2.5–3rem section space</td><td>Reference-page defaults. Group related items first; adapt density to the reading task.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Display type</th><td>Tilt Prism at 40px or larger</td><td>Use Instrument Sans below that threshold. Data uses Spline Sans Mono.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></section><section id="components" class="guide-chapter" aria-labelledby="components-title"><header><div><p class="prism-eyebrow">04 / COMPONENTS</p><h2 id="components-title">The rules become a family.</h2></div><p>Choose permanence, then role, then size. These specimens share the same edge, shadow, color, and motion definitions; each is a combination of the model.</p></header><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-density">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">LAW 5 · DENSITY</span>
          <h3 id="ds-density">Density — permanence condenses light into pigment</h3>
          <p>
            The more permanent a component, the more its color condenses: transient lives on
            the edge and glow; standing earns a tinted wash and a 1px border; immovable is
            opaque pigment with near-zero shadow. Destructive actions live in the immovable
            tier — no spectral play.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--3">
          <div class="prism-card ds-lift">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Transient</p>
            <p class="prism-body" style="color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0.4rem 0 1rem;">Seconds. Edge + spectral glow only.</p>
            <button class="prism-btn prism-btn--primary prism-edge" type="button">Primary action</button>
          </div>

          <div class="prism-card" style="border: 1px solid var(--hairline); background: var(--standing-tint); color:var(--on-ground); box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Standing</p>
            <p class="prism-body" style="color: inherit; margin: 0.4rem 0 1rem;">Session. Tinted wash + 1px border, short shadow.</p>
            <button class="prism-btn" type="button" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">Secondary</button>
          </div>

          <div class="prism-card" style="background: var(--dense); color: var(--on-dense); box-shadow: var(--elev-dense);">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Immovable</p>
            <p class="prism-body" style="opacity: 0.85; margin: 0.4rem 0 1rem;">Structural. Opaque pigment, compact, no spectra.</p>
            <div class="ds-row-actions">
              <button class="prism-btn prism-btn--dense" type="button">Save</button>
              <button class="prism-btn prism-btn--dense prism-btn--danger" type="button">Delete</button>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-matrix">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">SIZES × STYLES</span>
          <h3 id="ds-matrix">The set, laid out</h3>
          <p>
            Five button sizes over four styles, three chip sizes over two, and the
            role-token names and live swatches. Brand treatments derive from the
            anchor hue, while semantic hues stay fixed. The sheet updates when you drag the slider — and
            every one of them inverts on its own terms at night, using the night values of the same role tokens.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="prism-card" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense)">
          <p class="prism-eyebrow">Button · 5 sizes / 4 styles</p>
          <div class="ds-matrix" style="margin-top: 1rem">
            <span class="ds-mx-label"></span>
            {['xs', 'sm', 'md', 'lg', 'xl'].map((n) => (
              <span class="ds-mx-label ds-mx-head">{n}</span>
            ))}
            {[
              ['primary', 'prism-btn--primary prism-edge'],
              ['secondary', ''],
              ['dense', 'prism-btn--dense'],
              ['danger', 'prism-btn--dense prism-btn--danger'],
            ].map(([label, style]) => (
              <>
                <span class="ds-mx-label">{label}</span>
                {['prism-btn--xs', 'prism-btn--sm', '', 'prism-btn--lg', 'prism-btn--xl'].map((size) => (
                  <span class="ds-mx-cell">
                    <button class={`prism-btn ${style} ${size}`} type="button">Deploy</button>
                  </span>
                ))}
              </>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--2" style="margin-top: 1rem; align-items: start">
          <div class="prism-card" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense)">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Badge &amp; chip · 3 sizes / 2 styles</p>
            <div class="ds-stack" style="margin-top: 1rem; gap: 0.9rem">
              {['prism-badge--sm', '', 'prism-badge--lg'].map((size, i) => (
                <div class="ds-row-actions">
                  <span class={`prism-badge prism-badge--ok ${size}`}>✓ Approved</span>
                  <span class={`prism-badge prism-badge--no ${size}`}>✕ Rejected</span>
                  <span class={`prism-badge prism-badge--warn ${size}`}>! Pending</span>
                  <span class="ds-mx-label">{['sm', 'md', 'lg'][i]}</span>
                </div>
              ))}
              {['prism-chip--sm', '', 'prism-chip--lg'].map((size, i) => (
                <div class="ds-row-actions">
                  <span class={`prism-chip prism-edge ${size}`}><i class="prism-dot"></i>anchor</span>
                  <span class={`prism-chip prism-edge ${size}`}>OKLCH</span>
                  <span class={`prism-chip prism-edge ${size}`}>q = 40ms</span>
                  <span class="ds-mx-label">chip · {['sm', 'md', 'lg'][i]}</span>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="prism-card" style="box-shadow: var(--elev-dense)">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Role tokens · live</p>
            <div class="ds-tokens" style="margin-top: 1rem">
              {[
                ['--anchor', 'brand +0°'], ['--near', 'brand −35°'],
                ['--far', 'brand +75°'], ['--counter', 'brand −140°'],
                ['--field', 'ground'], ['--surface', 'paper'],
                ['--ink', 'text'], ['--dense', 'immovable'],
                ['--success', 'fixed 152°'], ['--danger', 'fixed 27°'],
                ['--warning', 'fixed 75°'], ['--hairline', 'boundary'],
              ].map(([tok, role]) => (
                <div class="ds-token">
                  <span class="ds-token-chip" style={`background: var(${tok})`}></span>
                  <span class="ds-token-name">{tok}</span>
                  <span class="ds-token-role">{role}</span>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
            <p class="ds-caption" style="margin-top: 1rem">
              Four brand bands at fixed angular offsets, three semantics pinned to
              their hue angles, and one hairline. Everything else on this page is
              built from those.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-displays">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">LAW 22 · ICONS &amp; DISPLAYS</span>
          <h3 id="ds-displays">Icons &amp; displays — quantized light on tiny screens</h3>
          <p>
            After Ink &amp; Switch's <a href="https://www.inkandswitch.com/project/tenfold/playground/">Tenfold</a>:
            icons live on coarse 8×8 displays that <em>emit</em> rather than print, so the
            glass stays dark in both modes and the emitted color follows its band. Click an icon to
            re-tune its band (anchor → near → far → counter). And in Tenfold's spirit —
            click and drag everywhere: the big display is paintable.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--2" style="align-items: start;">
          <div>
            <div class="ds-icon-row" aria-label="Icon displays">
              {glyphs.map((g) => (
                <button
                  class="ds-icon-tile"
                  type="button"
                  data-cycle
                  data-name={g.name}
                  aria-label={`${g.name} display — band anchor, click to re-tune`}
                >
                  <span class="prism-screen" data-band="anchor" aria-hidden="true">
                    {g.rows.join('').split('').map((c) => (
                      <b class={c === '#' ? 'on' : undefined}></b>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="prism-data">{g.name}</span>
                </button>
              ))}
            </div>
            <p class="ds-caption">
              One-bit sprites, static DOM. The sheen crossing each screen is the same
              reference beam — no display owns a listener.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <div
              id="ds-paint"
              class="prism-screen ds-paint"
              data-band="anchor"
              style={`--cols: ${paintCols}; --px-size: 16px; --px-gap: 3px;`}
              role="application"
              tabindex="0"
              aria-label="Paint display. Drag to paint pixels; drag from a lit pixel to erase. Arrow keys move the cursor, Enter or Space toggles a pixel."
            >
              {paintSeed.join('').split('').map((c) => (
                <b class={c === '#' ? 'on' : undefined}></b>
              ))}
            </div>
            <div class="ds-row-actions" style="margin-top: 0.6rem;">
              <button id="ds-paint-clear" class="ds-replay" type="button">Clear</button>
              <p class="ds-caption" style="margin: 0;">16×10 · pixels emit the current anchor band.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section></section><section id="information" class="guide-chapter" aria-labelledby="information-title"><header><div><p class="prism-eyebrow">05 / INFORMATION PATTERNS</p><h2 id="information-title">Give light a reading order.</h2></div><p>A material treatment must help explain the information. Separate the overview from the evidence; separate selection from status. These are compositions of the same primitives.</p></header><div class="guide-composition"><div class="prism-card prism-edge"><p class="prism-eyebrow">PATTERN / SUMMARY</p><h3>One release needs attention.</h3><p>Two checks are ready. One needs a decision. Summarize the state before asking someone to inspect the records.</p><p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><span class="prism-badge prism-badge--warn">! Review required</span></p><p class="ds-caption">Sample content. The card floats above the records; the status is named explicitly.</p></div>
<div class="guide-table-wrap"><table class="guide-table"><caption>Pattern / comparison and decision</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Next step</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr class="guide-record"><th scope="row">Typography</th><td>✓ Ready</td><td>Keep scale</td></tr><tr class="guide-record" data-current="true"><th scope="row">Focus states<small>Current review</small></th><td>! Review</td><td>Inspect ring</td></tr><tr class="guide-record"><th scope="row">Motion</th><td>✓ Ready</td><td>Keep timing</td></tr>
</tbody></table><details><summary>Why a seam instead of a glow?</summary><p>The shared ground keeps rows comparable. A narrow seam identifies the current record without making every row float. The label still identifies it when color is unavailable.</p></details></div></div>
<div class="guide-invariant"><strong>THE INFORMATION RULE</strong><p>At low density, a cast establishes a focal object. At high density, alignment and hairlines establish relationships. Use the same material vocabulary at a quieter intensity as the amount of information grows.</p></div><section class="ds-section" aria-labelledby="ds-elevation">
        <header>
          <span class="ds-law">LAW 6–7 · ELEVATION</span>
          <h3 id="ds-elevation">Elevation — cast floats, hairline grounds</h3>
          <p>
            Day shadows are 3-layer chromatic casts thrown along the light axis (135° at
            rest — move the pointer and every cast on the page swings away from it);
            night shadows are axis-free halos. Shadows read chromatic tokens. Inside data, casts are forbidden —
            elevation collapses to 1px hairlines, and refraction enters only as a 2px seam on
            the hovered row. A cast now <em>means</em> "floats above the data."
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="ds-grid ds-grid--2" style="align-items: start;">
          <div class="prism-card ds-lift prism-edge">
            <p class="prism-eyebrow">Floating card</p>
            <p class="prism-body" style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0; color: var(--ink-soft);">
              Full cast (day) / halo (night). Hover to lift — at night it glows brighter
              instead, because there's nothing to lift toward.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <div class="prism-table" role="table" aria-label="Dense data (hairline elevation)" style="--cols: 1fr 1fr 1fr;">
              <div class="ds-thead" role="row">
                <span role="columnheader">Run</span><span role="columnheader">Hue</span><span role="columnheader">State</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prism-row" role="row" tabindex="0"><span>0xA1</span><span>185°</span><span>ok</span></div>
              <div class="prism-row" role="row" tabindex="0" aria-selected="true"><span>0xA2</span><span>150°</span><span>ok</span></div>
              <div class="prism-row" role="row" tabindex="0"><span>0xA3</span><span>027°</span><span>fail</span></div>
              <div class="prism-row" role="row" tabindex="0"><span>0xA4</span><span>075°</span><span>warn</span></div>
            </div>
            <p class="ds-caption">No cast inside the table. Hover a row → 2px left seam.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section></section><section id="use" class="guide-chapter" aria-labelledby="use-title"><header><div><p class="prism-eyebrow">06 / BUILD WITH IT</p><h2 id="use-title">A recipe, with boundaries.</h2></div><p>Use the material classes to construct the interface. Keep the model visible in your choices, then verify the resulting composition.</p></header><div class="ds-grid ds-grid--2"><div><h3>Load the material layer.</h3><pre class="guide-code" tabindex="0" aria-label="Prismatic starter markup"><code>{`<html data-mode="day" style="--h:185; --hr:129">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/prism/prism.css">

<article class="prism-card prism-edge">
  <h2>Review the release</h2>
  <p>A quiet surface for the decision.</p>
  <button class="prism-btn prism-btn--dense">
    Inspect release
  </button>
</article>
</html>`}</code></pre><p>Load the CSS for the resting material. A hue control must also update the derived night hue. The pointer driver is optional; static light values are built in.</p></div><div><h3>Know what to avoid.</h3><ul class="guide-avoid"><li><strong>Independent colors</strong><p>Derive brand bands from the anchor; use fixed semantic roles for state.</p></li><li><strong>Glow on every row</strong><p>Dense information needs alignment, hairlines, and a narrow selection seam.</p></li><li><strong>Every object floats</strong><p>Use density to distinguish a temporary surface from a structural action.</p></li><li><strong>Color as the only message</strong><p>Keep status labels, visible focus, and a reduced-motion path.</p></li></ul><p>Check the foreground on its actual ground in both modes. The body-text audit covers selected pairs and hues; it does not certify every gradient, button, or application layout.</p></div></div><div class="ds-row-actions" style="margin-top:1.5rem"><a href="/prism/prism.tokens.json" download>Token reference ↓</a><a href="/prism/prism.css" download>Material stylesheet ↓</a><a href="/design/void">Read VOID ↗</a><a href="/design/acrylics">Read Acrylics ↗</a></div></section>
    </main>

    <footer class="ds-footer">
      <p>
        PRISM v0.7 · all color OKLCH · tokens:
        <a href="/prism/prism.tokens.json">prism.tokens.json</a> · layer:
        <a href="/prism/prism.css">prism.css</a>
      </p>
      <div class="ds-audit">
        <strong>Contrast audit (WCAG AA, body ink on field):</strong><br />
        Verified across hues <code>{`{20, 60, 90, 150, 185, 260, 310}`}</code> in both modes via
        <code>scripts/prism-contrast-audit.mjs</code>. The yellow-green band
        (<code>60–90°</code>) is the known risk zone; <code>--ink</code> holds AA there because
        its lightness (L&nbsp;0.28 day / paper-dark night) keeps the ratio above 4.5:1 across the
        wheel — no ink-darkening fix was required. See the script output for per-hue ratios.
      </div>
    </footer>

    <script is:inline>
      (function () {
        const root = document.documentElement;
        const slider = document.getElementById('ds-hue-slider');
        const dayH = document.getElementById('ds-day-h');
        const nightH = document.getElementById('ds-night-h');
        const modeBtn = document.getElementById('ds-mode-toggle');
        const modeLabel = document.getElementById('ds-mode-label');
        const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');

        // Redshift: slide hue toward the long-wavelength (red) end. Spectral hues
        // (45–300) descend toward red (45); non-spectral pinks (300–360) wrap forward.
        function prismRedshift(h, k = 0.4) {
          if (h <= 45) return h;
          if (h > 300) return Math.round((h + (385 - h) * k) % 360);
          return Math.round(h - (h - 45) * k);
        }

        function applyHue(h) {
          root.style.setProperty('--h', h);
          const hr = prismRedshift(h);
          root.style.setProperty('--hr', hr);
          dayH.textContent = h + '°';
          nightH.textContent = hr + '°';
          const night = root.dataset.mode === 'night';
          const offsets = night ? [0, -25, 55, -95] : [0, -35, 75, -140];
          const base = night ? hr : h;
          const bands = offsets.map(offset => ((base + offset) % 360 + 360) % 360);
          document.getElementById('guide-hue-equation').textContent =
            (night ? 'Night' : 'Day') + ': H = ' + base + '° → bands ' + bands.map(band => band + '°').join(', ');
          document.querySelectorAll('[data-band-offset]').forEach(function (label) {
            const offset = offsets[Number(label.dataset.bandOffset)];
            label.textContent = (offset >= 0 ? '+' : '−') + Math.abs(offset) + '°';
          });
        }

        slider.addEventListener('input', function (e) { applyHue(parseInt(e.target.value, 10)); });
        applyHue(parseInt(slider.value, 10));

        // Mode toggle — refract over 25q (1000ms); never snap.
        modeBtn.addEventListener('click', function () {
          const next = root.dataset.mode === 'night' ? 'day' : 'night';
          root.dataset.mode = next;
          modeLabel.textContent = next === 'night' ? 'Redshift' : 'Day';
          modeBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(next === 'night'));
          applyHue(parseInt(slider.value, 10));
        });

        // Replay helper: re-trigger an entrance animation by reflow.
        function replay(el) {
          el.style.animation = 'none';
          el.querySelectorAll('*').forEach(function (n) { n.style.animation = 'none'; });
          void el.offsetWidth; // force reflow
          el.style.animation = '';
          el.querySelectorAll('*').forEach(function (n) { n.style.animation = ''; });
        }

        document.querySelectorAll('[data-replay]').forEach(function (btn) {
          btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
            const kind = btn.getAttribute('data-replay');
            if (kind === 'stagger') replay(document.getElementById('ds-stagger'));
            if (kind === 'enter') {
              const host = document.getElementById('ds-enter').firstElementChild;
              host.classList.remove('prism-enter');
              void host.offsetWidth;
              host.classList.add('prism-enter');
            }
            if (kind === 'collapse') collapse();
          });
        });

        // Superposition collapse + reset (toggles on the Observe button).
        const ghosts = document.querySelectorAll('[data-ghost]');
        const collapsed = document.getElementById('ds-collapsed');
        let isCollapsed = false;
        function collapse() {
          isCollapsed = !isCollapsed;
          ghosts.forEach(function (g) { g.classList.toggle('collapsed', isCollapsed); });
          collapsed.hidden = !isCollapsed;
          const btn = document.querySelector('[data-replay="collapse"]');
          if (btn) btn.textContent = isCollapsed ? 'Superpose' : 'Observe';
        }

        // Entanglement: light all elements sharing a datum with Δt = 0.
        function setLit(pair, on) {
          document.querySelectorAll('[data-pair="' + pair + '"]').forEach(function (el) {
            el.classList.toggle('lit', on);
          });
        }
        document.querySelectorAll('[data-pair]').forEach(function (el) {
          const pair = el.getAttribute('data-pair');
          el.addEventListener('mouseenter', function () { setLit(pair, true); });
          el.addEventListener('mouseleave', function () { setLit(pair, false); });
          el.addEventListener('focus', function () { setLit(pair, true); });
          el.addEventListener('blur', function () { setLit(pair, false); });
        });

        // ===== HOLOGRAPHY — reference-cascade light driver =====
        // The pointer is the light. Six registered refs on :root are the ONLY
        // thing this driver ever writes — one rAF-batched write per frame at
        // most, and a ref is written only when it crosses its quantum, so most
        // pointer frames write nothing. Everything downstream is var()/calc().
        const holo = { la: 135, lvx: 0.7071, lvy: 0.7071, li: 0.35, lxp: 30, lyp: 20 };
        let contLa = 135; // unwound angle: 359°→1° must not spin the page backwards
        let lpx = 0, lpy = 0, lraf = 0, frames = 0, writes = 0;
        const refEls = {
          la: document.getElementById('ds-ref-la'),
          v: document.getElementById('ds-ref-v'),
          li: document.getElementById('ds-ref-li'),
          frames: document.getElementById('ds-ref-frames'),
          writes: document.getElementById('ds-ref-writes'),
        };

        function quant(v, step) { return Math.round(Math.round(v / step) * step * 100) / 100; }

        function lightFrame() {
          lraf = 0; frames++;
          const w = window.innerWidth, hgt = window.innerHeight;
          let dx = w / 2 - lpx, dy = hgt / 2 - lpy; // cast direction: away from the light
          const len = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;
          dx /= len; dy /= len;
          const ang = (Math.atan2(dx, -dy) * 180 / Math.PI + 360) % 360;
          const delta = ((ang - (((contLa % 360) + 360) % 360) + 540) % 360) - 180;
          contLa += delta;
          const next = {
            la: quant(contLa, 3),
            lvx: quant(dx, 0.02),
            lvy: quant(dy, 0.02),
            li: quant(Math.min(1, 0.2 + (len / Math.max(w, hgt)) * 0.9), 0.05),
            lxp: quant((lpx / w) * 100, 2),
            lyp: quant((lpy / hgt) * 100, 2),
          };
          let changed = false;
          for (const k in next) {
            if (next[k] !== holo[k]) {
              holo[k] = next[k];
              root.style.setProperty('--' + k, String(next[k]));
              changed = true;
            }
          }
          if (changed) writes++; // one batched write, ≤1 per frame
          if (changed && refEls.la) {
            refEls.la.textContent = String(((Math.round(holo.la) % 360) + 360) % 360);
            refEls.v.textContent = holo.lvx.toFixed(2) + ', ' + holo.lvy.toFixed(2);
            refEls.li.textContent = holo.li.toFixed(2);
            refEls.frames.textContent = String(frames);
            refEls.writes.textContent = String(writes);
          }
        }

        if (!reduce.matches) {
          window.addEventListener('pointermove', function (e) {
            lpx = e.clientX; lpy = e.clientY;
            if (!lraf) lraf = requestAnimationFrame(lightFrame);
          }, { passive: true });
        }

        // ===== ICONS — click to re-tune a display's emission band =====
        const bands = ['anchor', 'near', 'far', 'counter'];
        document.querySelectorAll('[data-cycle]').forEach(function (btn) {
          btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
            const screen = btn.querySelector('.prism-screen');
            const band = bands[(bands.indexOf(screen.getAttribute('data-band')) + 1) % bands.length];
            screen.setAttribute('data-band', band);
            btn.setAttribute('aria-label', btn.getAttribute('data-name') + ' display — band ' + band + ', click to re-tune');
          });
        });

        // ===== PAINT DISPLAY — click and drag everywhere (after Tenfold) =====
        const paint = document.getElementById('ds-paint');
        if (paint) {
          const cells = Array.prototype.slice.call(paint.children);
          const COLS = 16, ROWS = 10;
          let painting = false, paintOn = true, cur = 0;

          function cellAt(e) {
            const c0 = cells[0].getBoundingClientRect();
            const pitchX = cells[1].getBoundingClientRect().left - c0.left;
            const pitchY = cells[COLS].getBoundingClientRect().top - c0.top;
            const col = Math.floor((e.clientX - c0.left) / pitchX);
            const row = Math.floor((e.clientY - c0.top) / pitchY);
            if (col < 0 || col >= COLS || row < 0 || row >= ROWS) return null;
            return cells[row * COLS + col];
          }
          paint.addEventListener('pointerdown', function (e) {
            const c = cellAt(e);
            if (!c) return;
            painting = true;
            paintOn = !c.classList.contains('on');
            c.classList.toggle('on', paintOn);
            paint.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
            e.preventDefault();
          });
          paint.addEventListener('pointermove', function (e) {
            if (!painting) return;
            const c = cellAt(e);
            if (c) c.classList.toggle('on', paintOn);
          });
          ['pointerup', 'pointercancel'].forEach(function (t) {
            paint.addEventListener(t, function () { painting = false; });
          });
          // keyboard path: arrows move a cursor pixel, Enter/Space toggles it
          paint.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
            const step = { ArrowLeft: -1, ArrowRight: 1, ArrowUp: -COLS, ArrowDown: COLS }[e.key];
            if (step !== undefined) {
              cells[cur].classList.remove('cursor');
              cur = Math.max(0, Math.min(cells.length - 1, cur + step));
              cells[cur].classList.add('cursor');
              e.preventDefault();
            } else if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {
              cells[cur].classList.toggle('on');
              e.preventDefault();
            }
          });
          paint.addEventListener('focus', function () { cells[cur].classList.add('cursor'); });
          paint.addEventListener('blur', function () { cells[cur].classList.remove('cursor'); });
          document.getElementById('ds-paint-clear').addEventListener('click', function () {
            cells.forEach(function (c) { c.classList.remove('on'); });
          });
        }
      })();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
